    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Where do you see the horizontal asymptote of the graph in the expressions for the functions?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Consider the function \\(a(x) = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}x+1}{x-1}\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Predict where you think the vertical and horizontal asymptotes of \\(a(x)\\) will be. Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use graphing technology to check your prediction.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Consider the rational function \\(f(x) = \\frac{3x+1}{x}\\). Written this way, we can tell that the graph of the function has a vertical asymptote at \\(x=0\\) by reading the denominator and identifying the value that would cause division by…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "One way we can think about these values is to rewrite the expression for \\(f(x)\\) by breaking up the fraction:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(f(x) = \\frac{3x}{x} + \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ f(x)= 3 + \\frac{1}{x}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Written this way, it’s easier to see that as \\(x\\) gets larger and larger in either the positive or negative direction, the \\(\\frac{1}{x}\\) term will get closer and closer to 0. Because of this, we can say that the value of the function…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "More generally, if a rational function \\(g(x) = \\frac{a(x)}{b(x)}\\) can be rewritten as \\(g(x) = c + \\frac{r(x)}{b(x)}\\), where \\(c\\) is a constant, and \\(r(x)\\) and \\(b(x)\\) are polynomial expressions where \\(\\frac{r(x)}{b(x)}\\) gets…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Rational functions of this type have a horizontal asymptote at the constant value. The line \\(y=c\\) is a horizontal asymptote for \\(f\\) if \\(f(x)\\) gets closer and closer to \\(c\\) as the magnitude of \\(x\\) increases.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The line \\(y =c\\) is a horizontal asymptote of a function if the outputs of the function get closer and closer to \\(c\\) as the inputs get larger and larger in either the positive or negative direction. This means the graph gets closer and…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "horizontal asymptote",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A rational function is a function defined by a fraction with polynomials in the numerator and denominator. Rational functions include polynomials because a polynomial can be written as a fraction with denominator 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L18-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "18.3: Horizontal Asymptotes",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The line \\(x=a\\) is a vertical asymptote for a function \\(f\\) if \\(f\\) is undefined at \\(x=a\\) and its outputs get larger and larger in the negative or positive direction when \\(x\\) gets closer and closer to \\(a\\) on each side of the line.…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
